`]}),e.jsxs(r,{title:"Pairs, frequencies and the extension ratio",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Formula (1) gives the rate at which each pair turns. The ",e.jsx(a,{math:"d_r",display:!1})," rotated coordinates of a head form ",e.jsx(a,{math:"d_r/2",display:!1})," pairs, so ",e.jsx(a,{math:"d_r",display:!1})," is even. The pairs are indexed ",e.jsx(a,{math:"j = 0, \\dots, d_r/2 - 1",display:!1}),", and each rotates as a two-dimensional vector. At position ",e.jsx(a,{math:"m",display:!1}),", pair ",e.jsx(a,{math:"j",display:!1})," has the angle ",e.jsx(a,{math:"m\\omega_j",display:!1}),". The base ",e.jsx(a,{math:"b",display:!1})," is not itself a frequency. It sets every pair's frequency through this formula. Code calls ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\omega_j",display:!1})," the inverse frequency because it is the reciprocal of ",e.jsx(a,{math:"b^{2j/d_r}",display:!1}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Week 3 showed that the rotary part of the score between a query at position ",e.jsx(a,{math:"m",display:!1})," and a key at position ",e.jsx(a,{math:"n",display:!1})," depends on the two positions only through the signed offset ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\Delta = n - m",display:!1}),". In causal attention a query sees only keys at or before its own position, so ",e.jsx(a,{math:"n \\le m",display:!1})," and ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\Delta \\le 0",display:!1}),". A range of offsets is therefore written as a signed interval, such as ",e.jsx(a,{math:"-(L_0 - 1) \\le \\Delta \\le 0",display:!1})," within training, or as a bound on ",e.jsx(a,{math:"|\\Delta|",display:!1}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The extension ratio ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\rho = L_t / L_0 \\ge 1",display:!1})," divides the target length by the trained length. Serving an 8K-trained model at 32K means ",e.jsx(a,{math:"\\rho = 4",display:!1}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Write ",e.jsx(a,{math:"D_h",display:!1})," for the head dimension. With full rotary, ",e.jsx(a,{math:"d_r = D_h",display:!1})," and every coordinate of the head rotates. With partial rotary, ",e.jsx(a,{math:"D_h - d_r",display:!1})," coordinates stay unrotated. An attention logit sums over the coordinates of the head, so with partial rotary it splits into a part from the rotated coordinates and a part from the unrotated ones."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The frequency falls as ",e.jsx(a,{math:"j",display:!1})," grows. Low-index pairs rotate quickly: their wavelength is short, the same offset changes their angle a lot, and they complete many turns within the training length. High-index pairs rotate slowly: their wavelength is long, the same offset changes their angle little, and they complete few turns. Every method in this chapter changes how these bands behave when ",e.jsx(a,{math:"L_t > L_0",display:!1}),"."]})]})]})]}),`
